      "content": "In the *Vaughan v. Maritime Logistics Ltd [2022]* case, the High Court's decision hinged on the defendant's failure to maintain traction sensors on cargo vessels, which was deemed a breach of the duty of care as stipulated by the Merchant Shipping Act 1995. The court's reasoning was anchored in the precedent set by *Donoghue v Stevenson [1932]*, which established the foundational principle of duty of care. The judgment further dissected the three-stage test from *Caparo Industries plc v Dickman [1990]*, which assesses duty, breach, and causation, to meticulously apply these criteria to the facts of the case. Additionally, the court referenced *Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire Police [2018]* to demonstrate how established precedents are applied to negligence duties in maritime contexts. The court concluded that Maritime Logistics Ltd's negligence in maintaining the traction sensors directly led to the incident, emphasizing the critical nature of adhering to safety protocols in maritime operations. This comprehensive analysis, supported by relevant case law, underscores the court's commitment to upholding the duty of care in maritime negligence cases.",
